00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.620
NATHEN HARVEY:
Next 2019 included

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.280
so many great sessions.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.820
It is likely you missed
a few or wanted to relive

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.230
some of your favorites.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.510
My friend Seth Vargo
gave an awesome talk

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.430
about DevOps vs. SRE.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.010
Are they competing
standards or friends?

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.055
Join me for a spoiler-free
preview of Seth's presentation.

00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:19.865
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:26.440
Developers are told to build
and ship features faster.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.350
Operators are told to keep the
system stable and reliable.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.970
This creates an environment
with misaligned incentives.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.220
This misalignment
encourages developers

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.370
to throw changes over the
proverbial wall as fast

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.860
as possible, while
encouraging operators

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.020
to reject those
changes in pursuit

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.780
of stability and reliability.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.420
Shipping faster and running
more reliable systems

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.920
are great objectives.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.020
But the DevOps movement
is about more than that.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.770
It's about aligning everyone
to improve the customer

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.180
experience.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.370
There are a number
of DevOps practices

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.480
that we can put
in place to help.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.239
Let's take a quick
look at some of them.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.000
First up is the
idea that we should

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.310
reduce organizational silos.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.860
Developers and operators
working together

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.490
are more capable of
delivering the best results.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.440
We all want to live
in a world where

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.610
our systems are 100% available,
reliable, and performant.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.900
We also know that this land
of unicorns and lollipops

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.880
does not actually exist.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.970
DevOps implores us
to accept failure

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.520
as a normal mode of operation.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.760
Something we can do to
improve both speed and safety

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.090
is implement and introduce
changes gradually.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.310
Deploying a small
change that was made

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.000
is much safer than deploying
a large change that

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.880
was made and has been building
up over a number of weeks

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.620
or even months.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.870
Another practice of
DevOps is leveraging

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.040
tooling and automation.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.460
Whether provisioning systems,
deploying applications,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.470
or patching the
operating system,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.980
there is a place for good
automation practices.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.620
Making these tasks
easy and automatic

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.530
frees up developers,
operators, and others

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.650
to focus on more important work.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.510
Finally, DevOps teaches us that
we need to measure everything.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.810
Having good metrics and
measurements about our systems

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.760
helps us make good
decisions about how

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.680
to improve those systems.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.450
Without good
metrics, how will we

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.310
know if our changes
were impactful?

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.450
So those are some of
the DevOps practices.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.860
What, exactly, is SRE?

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.090
Well, in 2016, Google,
together with O'Reilly,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.780
released a book on the topic of
site reliability engineering.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.610
The ideas presented
in the book have

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.480
led some to ask the question
that is central to this talk--

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.870
are DevOps and SRE
competitors or friends?

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.560
One concept that SRE
introduces is the error budget.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.570
It helps the business
make trade offs

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.150
between speed and reliability.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.690
The error budget informs what
to do when the system is not

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.730
meeting its reliability goals.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.890
For example, the
error budget may

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.010
dictate that teams stop
pushing new features

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.320
and focus all
engineering efforts

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.050
on the things that make
the system more reliable,

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.820
like automated deployments.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.420
SRE provides a framework for
defining these error budgets.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.230
We start with service
level indicators, or SLIs.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.980
An SLI is a metric that tells
us something about the system.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.350
The best SLIs are
the ones that give us

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.690
an indication of
customer happiness.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.000
So for example, a
customer will be happy

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.180
when their shopping cart loads
correctly and unhappy when

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.920
it loses items.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:42.880
From the SLIs, we can now
generate some service level

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.250
objectives, or SLOs.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.900
An SLO lets us set a goal for
an acceptable level of service

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.190
reliability over time.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.230
For example, we might say
that 99.99% of requests

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.350
to the shopping cart
over the past 28 days

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.260
should be successful.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.970
So DevOps and SRE--

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.520
are they competing
standards or friends?

